<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Execution-time ceiling for every query on this server.

      The stock default is max_execution_time = 0: no limit at all. On 2026-09-26
      that let `SELECT count() FROM system.numbers LIMIT 1000` (an aggregate over an
      infinite source; the LIMIT applies to the one result row, never to the input)
      run for 189.6 hours on one core, reading trillions of rows, until it was found
      and killed by hand. It had been started by a shell whose caller had long since
      timed out and gone away, so nothing on the client side was ever going to stop
      it. The same shape recurs whenever a session is abandoned mid-query.

      Every service connects as the one `default` user, so the profile below is the
      floor under all of them, and the numbers are set from what actually runs
      (system.query_log, 14 days to 2026-09-26, ~4.4M queries/day):

        client                          own setting   slowest observed
        api / api-public / api-data     20 s          20 s cap; overshoots to 89 s
                                                      because the check is per block
        derivations (long-op client)    3600 s        13.6 s (INSERT SELECT), 5.7 s
        account affinity                5 s           4.2 s
        raw-live / main-live / snapshot (none)        3.8 s SELECT, 2.6 s INSERT
        async-insert flushes            inherited     2.5 s
        user-backup / contract-backup   (none)        73 ms
        schema-bootstrap                (none)        15 ms per statement
        ad-hoc clickhouse-client        (none)        152 s
        external readers                120 s CONST   (readonly_analyst, outside
                                                      the repo, unaffected here)

      600 s is four times the slowest legitimate statement that ran without a
      setting of its own and 150 times the slowest ingestion statement, so it
      changes nothing that runs today; it only bounds the abandoned query. A client
      that has a reason to run longer says so on the statement or the connection
      (clickhouse-js `clickhouse_settings.max_execution_time`, clickhouse-client
      `max_execution_time=N`), as the derivations client already does.

      The constraints are the part that makes the ceiling real. Without them, a
      client can set max_execution_time = 0 and be back to unlimited; the `min` of
      1 s refuses that value outright (SETTING_CONSTRAINT_VIOLATION), and the `max`
      of 4 hours is the hard limit no setting can raise, a comfortable multiple of
      the 1 hour the long-op client asks for. Constraints declared on the default
      profile are the server-wide defaults for every user, so a reader profile that
      pins its own CONST value keeps it (120 s lies inside the range).

      throw is the overflow mode already in force; it is restated so nobody flips it
      to `break`, which would return a truncated result as if it were complete.

      Not set, deliberately: max_execution_time_leaf is a distributed-query knob and
      this is one server; max_estimated_execution_time kills a query once its
      projected total exceeds the bound, but the projection needs a known input size,
      which an infinite source does not have, and it misjudges queries that stop at a
      LIMIT, so it would add false kills without catching the case above.

      Reloads without a restart: copy into the container's
      /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/ and run SYSTEM RELOAD CONFIG; a parse error is
      only reported there, never by the copy. Verify on a NEW connection:
      SELECT name, value, min, max FROM system.settings WHERE name = 'max_execution_time'.
    -->
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <max_execution_time>600</max_execution_time>
            <timeout_overflow_mode>throw</timeout_overflow_mode>
            <constraints>
                <max_execution_time>
                    <min>1</min>
                    <max>14400</max>
                </max_execution_time>
            </constraints>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
